From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 11:34:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vfio/igd: publish the host stolen base to firmware

The iGPU keeps firmware-programmed pointers into stolen memory - the GuC
WOPCM window and the RC6 context - that no BAR quirk can relocate, because
the hardware anchors them to its own physical stolen base rather than to
anything the guest driver programs. The driver writes only relative
offsets and sizes, which the hardware then adds to that fixed base.

The consequence is that guest stolen memory placed at an arbitrary address
leaves those accesses pointing at host physical addresses which, in the
guest's IOVA space, are ordinary guest RAM. The GPU then reads and writes
whatever the guest happens to keep there. A Linux guest shows it at boot:

  i915: [drm] *ERROR* Stolen reserved area [mem 0x50200000-0x503fffff]
        outside stolen memory [mem 0x78300000-0x7befffff]

and then runs without stolen memory.

Publish the host stolen base as etc/igd-stolen-base so the firmware can
reserve the guest stolen region at exactly that address. With guest and
host stolen coincident, every value the hardware emits is already correct
in the guest's IOVA space, nothing needs relocating, and the existing
relocation quirks reduce to identity. BDSM is emulated by the time the
file is written, so the host value is read from the device.

Gen11 and later only (BDSM at 0xC0). Firmware is free to ignore the file
and allocate anywhere, which restores the previous behaviour.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
---
 hw/vfio/igd.c | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/vfio/igd.c b/hw/vfio/igd.c
index 5e61607..3b15e37 100644
--- a/hw/vfio/igd.c
+++ b/hw/vfio/igd.c
@@ -783,6 +783,43 @@ static bool vfio_pci_igd_config_quirk(VFIOPCIDevice *vdev, Error **errp)
 
     trace_vfio_pci_igd_bdsm_enabled(vdev->vbasedev.name, (gms_size / MiB));
 
+    /*
+     * Publish the host stolen base so the firmware can reserve guest stolen
+     * memory at the very same physical address.
+     *
+     * The iGPU keeps firmware-programmed pointers into stolen memory (WOPCM,
+     * the RC6 context) that no BAR quirk can move: the hardware anchors them
+     * to its own physical stolen base. If guest stolen memory lands anywhere
+     * else, those accesses hit whatever the guest keeps at the host address.
+     * Reserving guest stolen memory at the host address makes the guest's
+     * IOVA space agree with what the hardware emits.
+     *
+     * BDSM is emulated by now, so read the host value from the device.
+     * Firmware that does not know this file allocates anywhere, as before.
+     */
+    if (gen >= 11 && gms_size) {
+        uint64_t host_bdsm = 0;
+
+        if (pread(vdev->vbasedev.fd, &host_bdsm, sizeof(host_bdsm),
+                  vdev->config_offset + IGD_BDSM_GEN11) == sizeof(host_bdsm)) {
+            host_bdsm = le64_to_cpu(host_bdsm) & ~0xfffULL;
+        } else {
+            host_bdsm = 0;
+        }
+        if (host_bdsm) {
+            uint64_t *stolen_base = g_malloc(sizeof(*stolen_base));
+
+            *stolen_base = cpu_to_le64(host_bdsm);
+            fw_cfg_add_file(fw_cfg_find(), "etc/igd-stolen-base",
+                            stolen_base, sizeof(*stolen_base));
+            info_report("IGD host stolen memory at 0x%" PRIx64
+                        ", published as etc/igd-stolen-base", host_bdsm);
+        } else {
+            warn_report("IGD: host stolen memory base unknown, not published; "
+                        "guest stolen memory will not be at the host address");
+        }
+    }
+
     return true;
 
 error:
